This directory is managed by Flask-Migrate and Alembic.

Create a migration after adding or changing SQLAlchemy models:

    flask --app backend/run.py db migrate -m "describe the schema change"

Review the generated migration carefully before applying it:

    flask --app backend/run.py db upgrade

Never edit an already-applied migration to change production schema history. Add a new migration instead.

## Initial multi-company schema

The first migration creates global users, companies, and company memberships.

## Company access boundary

The second migration creates a restricted, NOLOGIN PostgreSQL role named
`sales_dash_app`. It receives read-only access to the identity, company, and
membership tables. Row-level security is enabled and forced on all three tables:

- Users can read only their own account row.
- Users can read only their own membership rows.
- A company is visible only when the current user has an active membership.
- Missing user context returns no rows.
- The app role has no write policies yet, so writes are denied by default.

The backend helper `set_tenant_context` sets user and company identifiers with
transaction-local PostgreSQL settings and verifies that the user is active and
has an active membership before setting the selected company. The user ID must
come from server-verified authentication; it must never come directly from a
request parameter. Authentication is a later milestone, so this helper is not
wired to public routes yet.

The production connection login must be a non-superuser, must not own these
tables, and must not have BYPASSRLS. Grant that login membership in
`sales_dash_app`; never run the web application as the migration/database
administrator. PostgreSQL superusers bypass row-level security.

CI applies this migration to PostgreSQL 16 and runs restricted-role tests with
two companies. The tests also create a temporary tenant-owned probe table with
the same company_id RLS rule planned for future business tables. They verify
that no rows are visible without context, only Company A rows are visible after
selecting Company A, and selecting Company B is rejected without an active
membership. The probe table is test-only and is dropped after the tests. This
verifies the company-access boundary before any customer, product, or sales
tables are introduced.

Before applying migrations in production, take a database backup and review
the migration SQL and deployment role grants. Downgrade removes the policies,
RLS flags, and membership index; it deliberately leaves the NOLOGIN role in
place because a deployment login may already be a member of it.
